% A page a recording device could not hold whole is refused, not
% emitted short of what it could not hold.
%
% The record keeps the marking calls a page received and gives them back
% to be painted again. A mark it cannot hold makes it short of one, and
% the shortage sticks: every later mark is refused, every replay refuses,
% and the page is refused when it comes to be put out. That ladder exists
% because a page missing a mark looks like a page, so silence is the
% dangerous outcome.
%
% The ladder is only whole if every way of losing a mark reaches its
% bottom rung. A mark can also be lost in the device in front of the
% record: the device is handed something to put on the page, cannot
% write it down, and answers an error. The record is not told by that --
% it was never called -- so it goes on describing a page that no longer
% has the thing in it, and the page is put out short.
%
% This drives that from the outside. The image entry is the one the
% painter uses for a sampled image (data/paint.ps looks for
% /.recordimage on the device and hands over the picture whole rather
% than painting it a rectangle per sample), and it is called here
% directly, standing in for the painter, so that what the device is
% handed can be chosen. The glyph entry is the same case for text.
%
% Four runs, selected with -Dcase=:
%
%   /whole   a picture the device can write down. The page comes out
%            with it, which is what makes its absence visible in the
%            next run rather than a page nobody could tell apart.
%   /bare    no picture at all, which is the page the /short run comes
%            out as where the loss goes unreported.
%   /short   a picture whose decode table is withheld. The device
%            refuses it, and the page is short of it from that moment.
%   /glyph   a glyph placement naming no mask the record holds. Nothing
%            is painted for it either.
%
% Each run says what the device answered and what became of the page.
% Which of those answers is right is the wrapper's to judge, since it
% differs run by run.

/case where { pop case } { /whole } ifelse /case exch def

% The device's own entries are reached the way the machinery reaches
% them, through the device dictionary, and the record's readings through
% internaldict -- neither is in the program's namespace.
/intd 1183615869 internaldict def

% The picture. One component a sample, decoded to the three values the
% target's pixels take, placed at 20,20 in device space at one sample
% per pixel. Small: what is being shown is whether it is on the page,
% not what it looks like.
/W 24 def
/H 24 def
/rows [ H { W string 0 1 W 1 sub { 1 index exch 255 put } for } repeat ] def
/lut 768 string def
0 1 255 {
    /v exch def
    lut v 3 mul 255 put
    lut v 3 mul 1 add 0 put
    lut v 3 mul 2 add 0 put
} for

/offer {
    24 dict
    dup /w W put
    dup /ncomp 1 put
    dup /nat 3 put
    dup /rgbrows true put
    dup /xoff 20 put
    dup /xscale 1 put
    dup /yoff 20 put
    dup /yscale 1 put
    dup /cx0 0 put
    dup /cy0 0 put
    dup /cx1 612 put
    dup /cy1 792 put
    % The one thing the /short run withholds. Everything else about the
    % offer describes a picture, so what is refused is a picture the
    % caller was painting rather than a call that never carried one.
    case /short ne { dup /lut lut put } if
} def

/say { print (\n) print flush } def
/said { $error /errorname get 32 string cvs print (\n) print flush } def

% The mark that is on the page whatever happens, so that a page emitted
% short is a page with this and nothing else. It is not what the runs
% differ by; the picture is.
0 0 1 setrgbcolor
100 100 60 60 rectfill

case /bare eq {
    (OFFER NONE) say
}{
    case /glyph eq {
        % Standing in for the glyph painter, which reaches this entry
        % once per glyph it has drawn. A placement naming no mask the
        % record holds paints nothing when it comes to be played, so
        % what it stands for is a glyph the page has lost.
        mark {
            0 0 0 -1 30 30 DEVICE dup /.recordglyph get exec
            (OFFER RECORDED) say
        } stopped { (OFFER REFUSED ) print said } if cleartomark
    }{
        mark {
            offer rows DEVICE dup /.recordimage get exec
            (OFFER RECORDED) say
        } stopped { (OFFER REFUSED ) print said } if cleartomark
    } ifelse
} ifelse

% The marks the record holds and the pictures among them, which is what
% says whether the offer reached it at all.
(HELD ) print
DEVICE intd /.recordcost get exec pop exch
20 string cvs print ( ) print 20 string cvs print (\n) print flush

% The page's own line begins with a break: the default page semantics
% end their showpage banner without one, so a report printed straight
% after would be the tail of that line and no reader anchored to a line
% would find it.
mark { showpage (\nPAGE OUT) say }
stopped { (\nPAGE REFUSED ) print said } if cleartomark
